\section{Implementation}

\subsection{Introduction}
As the success of the gesture recognition unit is highly dependant on the quality of the data provided by the feature extraction module, it was important that the feature extraction provided consistent results. For this reason three different techniques were implemented in order to determine the type of extraction that would be used. These include parabolic and circular least-squares regression as well as an approach that uses changes in direction to determine feature segmentation. These are discussed in more detail below.

\subsection{Parabolic least-squares regression} 
The first method attempted was parabolic ordinary least-squares regression. In this case, a set of linear equations were solved in order to obtain the parameters \emph{a}, \emph{b} and \emph{c} for the following equation: $y = a + bx + cx^2$. This equation could then be used to describe a feature and its requisite information. The set of linear equations, where $x_i$ and $y_i$ refer to the \emph{x} and \emph{y} coordinates of each data point, \emph{n} to the number of data points and \emph{a}, \emph{b} and \emph{c} the parameters for the equation of a parabola, is shown below:
\begin{eqnarray*}
	\sum_{i=1}^n y_i & = & a \sum_{i=1}^n 1 + b \sum_{i=1}^n x_i + c \sum_{i=1}^n x_i^2 \\
	\sum_{i=1}^n x_i y_i & = & a \sum_{i=1}^n x_i + b \sum_{i=1}^n x_i^2 + c \sum_{i=1}^n x_i^3 \\
	\sum_{i=1}^n x_i^2 y_i & = & a \sum_{i=1}^n x_i^2 + b \sum_{i=1}^n x_i^3 + c \sum_{i=1}^n x_i^4
\end{eqnarray*}
These equations were solved so that \emph{a}, \emph{b} and \emph{c} could be calculated. Thus, as in order to calculate all three parameters, various combinations of summated data points are used, the regression can be calculated incrementally for each new data point. That is, recalculation is not neccessary when adding new data. This was done by storing the values for each summation combination. When a new data point was inserted, its values were added to the current summation value for each combination. Thus the addition of a data point was accomplished by updating the summation values and then simply inserting these into the regression equations in order to obtain the new \emph{a}, \emph{b} and \emph{c} values.

In order to determine whether a new feature should be started, a rolling error value, with a windows size of 5, was kept. The size of the window was chosen as a compromise between the robustness of the measure (that is, a single outlier should not cause a new feature to be started) and the distortion caused at the end of each feature due to erroneous values being included. That is, when using a rolling error, a number of points have to have a large enough error for the overall value to be sufficient to end the feature. If the size of the window is too large, this can cause distortions in the feature description. This value was updated with the error associated with each new touch point. The error for each touch point was calculated as follows, where ($x_i$, $y_i$) is the current touch point:
\begin{eqnarray*}
	y_{new} & = & c + bx_{i} + ax_{i}^2 \\
	y_{error} & = & y_{i} - y_{new}
\end{eqnarray*}
When this error rose above 5, the feature was ended and a new one started. This involved calculating the information required for each feature and reseting the summation values.

\subsubsection{Limitations}
One issue related to the use of parabolic fitting, is the inability to recognise vertical lines due to the nature of the function. That is, vertical lines produce parameter values that tend towards infinity. In order to compensate for this, these values were set to \texttt{FLT\_MAX}. However, this does not adequately address the problem, resulting in features being ended too early due to widely different results caused by these inaccurate parameter values. In addition, the shape of a parabola may not be appropriate for describing all features. This leads to premature feature termination, which increases the number of features within a gesture. 

In order to compensate for this, a more robust model, that of a circle, was attempted.

\subsection{Circular least-squares regression}
Unlike parabolic regression, a circular model allows for vertical features to be more easily and accurately described. In order to perform a regression to fit data to the equation of a circle ($R^2 = (x - x_c)^2 + (y - y_c)^2$), the proceeding steps were performed. Coordinates are first transformed from (\emph{x}, \emph{y}) to (\emph{u}, \emph{v}) coordinates as follows:
\begin{eqnarray*}
	u_i & = & x_i - \bar{x} \\
	v_i & = & y_i - \bar{y} \\
	\mbox{where} \\
	\bar{x} = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n x_i & \mbox{and} & \bar{y} = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n y_i
\end{eqnarray*}
The circle to be fitted has centre ($u_c$, $v_c$), with radius \emph{R}. For the purpose of the regression, $\alpha$ is set to equal $R^2$. The linear equations to be solved to determine the centre point are:
\begin{eqnarray*}
	u_c \sum_{i=1}^n u_i^2 + v_c \sum_{i=1}^n u_i v_i & = & \frac{1}{2}\left(\sum_{i=1}^n u_i^3 + \sum_{i=1}^n u_i v_i^2 \right)  \\
	u_c \sum_{i=1}^n u_i v_i + v_c \sum_{i=1}^n v_i^2 & = & \frac{1}{2}\left(\sum_{i=1}^n v_i^3 + \sum_{i=1}^n v_i u_i^2 \right)	
\end{eqnarray*}
$\alpha$ is determined as follows:
\begin{eqnarray*}
	\alpha = u_c^2 + v_c^2 + \frac{\sum\limits^{n}_{i=1} u_i^2 + \sum\limits^{n}_{i=1} v_i^2}{n}	
\end{eqnarray*}
Thus, once the regression has been completed, the centre point in (\emph{x}, \emph{y}) coordinates and the radius, \emph{R}, can be calculated as shown below:
\begin{eqnarray*}
		(x_c, y_c) & = & (u_c, v_c) + (\bar{x}, \bar{y}) \\
		R & = & \sqrt{\alpha}
\end{eqnarray*}

As described in the parabolic least-squares section, due to the fact that solving these equations relies on summated values, they can be calculated incrementally for each new touch point, thus avoiding recalculation when adding values. $\bar{x}$ and $\bar{y}$ were calculated anew for each touch point, however. This was done by dividing the summated values for $x$ and $y$ by $n$. Once again a rolling error with a windows size of 5 was used in order to determine whether to start a new feature. However, due to the fact that the circular regression was far more sensitive to slight deviations, the error bound was set to 15, rather than 5.

\subsubsection{Limitations}
Circular regressiin proved to be far more sensitive to small deviations than the parabolic regression. This meant that the parameter values could changed greatly with the inclusion of one or two outliers. Thus the error bound had to be increased. However, this also meant that the end of each feature contained more erroneous points than in the parabolic version. Attempts were made to handle this by setting the end point of the feature to an earlier position, so that these erroneous points were included in the next feature, rather than the previous one. However, while this did improve the results, the sensitive nature of the regression decreases the robustness of this implementation.

\subsection{Direction-based segmentation}
As very little descriptive information was required from each feature, the third method that was implemented used changes in direction as a segmentation technique, with each feature being interpreted as a straight-line. This was done by calculating the change in \emph{x} and \emph{y} between the current touch point and the one immediately preceding it. These values were then used to determine which direction, of eight, the difference was in. Figure~\ref{quad} illustrates the possible eight directions.

\begin{figure}
  \begin{center}
    \includegraphics [scale=0.2]{quadrants.pdf}

  \end{center}
  \caption{Demonstration of the eight possible directions used for segmentation. The black lines deliminate the boundries of each direction with the red numbers indicating the assigned values in each condition.}
  \label{quad}
\end{figure}

Two values were stored in order to record the current direction in \emph{x} and \emph{y}. Both the \emph{x} and \emph{y} variables could take on one of three values; 1, 0 or -1, depending on the current direction. The possible combinations are shown in Figure~\ref{quad}. In order to better capture horizontal or vertical lines, if the distance between two points in one direction was between 10 and -10, the segment was considered to lie along one of the axes.

When a new direction value was calculated, it was compared against the previous direction values. If the values were the same, the touch point was set as the current end point of the feature. If the values differed and the length of the current feature was greater than 100 pixels, the current feature was ended, and a new one started. The distance check was added so that outliers do not cause very short (and thus non-informative) features to be created.

An number of other attempts were made to help counteract the effect of outliers. One of these techniques was to extend the previous feature. When the end of a feature was found, the parameters of this feature were checked against those of the previous feature. If they were found to be similar enough, these two features were combined. This was done by checking the angle made by the line connecting the start point of the previous feature to the previous feature's end point with the angle formed by the line from the current feature's start point to the most recent divergent touch point. If the absolute differance between these angles was less than 20 degrees, the previous feature was extended. Figure~\ref{extend} illustrates this concept. If the differance was greater than 20 degrees, however, the current feature would be ended and a new one started with the most recent direction as the current direction.

\begin{figure}
  \begin{center}
    \includegraphics [scale=0.3]{extend.pdf}

  \end{center}
  \caption{Illustration of the technique used to combine similar sequential features. The touch points are shown in black. The red dots illustrate the starting points of the two respective features. \emph{a} and \emph{b} are the angles between each feature (red line) and the positive \emph{x}-axis. If the difference between \emph{a} and \emph{b} is less than 20 degrees, they will be combined into one feature.}
  \label{extend}
\end{figure}

This technique allows for a simplification in the feature extraction process. In addition, due to the limited number of possible features, it provides a more robust approach as well as simplifying the computation needed within the gesture engines.

\subsubsection{Limitations}
Due to the course-grained approach described above, this feature extraction technique is unable to differentiate between subtle changes in direction. This means that gestures have to be composed of well defined lines and curves. The same problem is present when considering small gestures as the features are not large enough to be consistently recognised. Thus, the use of this method places limitations on the types of possible gestures that are possible for use with the system.

